\title{Causal Loops in Category Theory:\\ A Concrete Non-Strict Monoidal Category of Parenthesizations}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We construct an explicit, fully elementary monoidal category whose tensor product fails to be
associative on the nose yet is repaired by a canonical invertible associator, and in which all
of the Mac Lane coherence data holds automatically. The objects are binary trees with labelled
leaves --- formal parenthesizations of words --- and a morphism between two trees is a proof
that they have the same underlying leaf-word. This category is \emph{thin} (at most one morphism
between any two objects) and a \emph{groupoid} (every morphism invertible). We prove: (i) a
general principle that \emph{any} tensor datum on a thin category automatically satisfies the
pentagon, triangle and naturality axioms, so coherence is free; (ii) the concrete realization
that $(a\otimes b)\otimes c$ and $a\otimes(b\otimes c)$ are genuinely distinct objects joined by
a unique associator, so the category is non-strict; and (iii) a strictification theorem: every
bracketing is canonically isomorphic to a right-nested normal form, two bracketings are
isomorphic iff they share a word, and the whole category is equivalent to the discrete category
of words. This is a hands-on model of the slogan \emph{a coherent loop-tolerant algebraic
structure is equivalent to a strict one}.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The associative law $(a\cdot b)\cdot c = a\cdot(b\cdot c)$ is usually an equation. In higher
category theory the two groupings become distinct objects, and the law is upgraded to a
specified invertible transformation --- the \emph{associator}. This is the defining move of a
\emph{monoidal category}: a tensor product associative and unital only up to coherent natural
isomorphism. The price is \emph{coherence}: Mac Lane's pentagon and triangle axioms, ensuring
all re-bracketings agree.

We isolate the phenomenon in its purest concrete form: a monoidal category built directly from
parenthesizations in which (1) associativity fails as a literal equality of objects, (2) the
failure is repaired by a canonical and in fact unique associator, and (3) all coherence is
automatic because the category is thin. We then show the structure collapses: it is
categorically equivalent to the discrete category of underlying words, a concrete instance of
Mac Lane's strictification theorem.

We think of a parenthesization as recording how a composite is grouped, and of the associator
as recording how composition loops back on itself under regrouping. The central claim is that
when these loops are coherent --- when re-association transformations are rigid enough to be
unique --- the loop closes harmlessly: travelling out and back returns the identity.

\section{Coherence from thinness}

\begin{definition}[Thin category]
A category $\mathcal C$ is \emph{thin} if for all objects $X,Y$ the hom-set $\mathcal C(X,Y)$
has at most one element; equivalently, any two parallel morphisms are equal. A thin category is
the same data as a preorder viewed as a category.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Every diagram commutes]\label{prop:comm}
In a thin category, any two morphisms with the same source and target are equal; consequently
every diagram commutes.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition. A diagram commutes iff certain parallel composites are equal, and
parallel morphisms are equal by thinness.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Isomorphisms are unique]\label{prop:isouniq}
In a thin category there is at most one isomorphism between any two objects.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Two isomorphisms have equal forward morphisms by thinness, and an isomorphism is determined by
its forward morphism.
\end{proof}

A \emph{monoidal structure datum} on $\mathcal C$ is a tensor bifunctor $\otimes$, a unit
$\mathbf 1$, and natural isomorphisms $\aa_{X,Y,Z}\colon (X\otimes Y)\otimes Z\cong
X\otimes(Y\otimes Z)$, $\lambda_X\colon \mathbf 1\otimes X\cong X$, $\rho_X\colon X\otimes\mathbf
1\cong X$, \emph{without} assuming the axioms.

\begin{theorem}[Coherence is free on a thin category]\label{thm:free}
Let $\mathcal C$ be thin with any monoidal structure datum. Then the datum automatically
satisfies the pentagon, the triangle, and all naturality conditions; $\mathcal C$ is a genuine
monoidal category.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each axiom asserts equality of two morphisms sharing a source and target. By
Proposition~\ref{prop:comm} every such equality holds automatically.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Pentagon]\label{cor:pent}
For all $W,X,Y,Z$,
\[
(\aa_{W,X,Y}\otimes \id_Z)\circ\aa_{W, X\otimes Y, Z}\circ(\id_W\otimes \aa_{X,Y,Z})
=\aa_{W\otimes X, Y, Z}\circ\aa_{W, X, Y\otimes Z}.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Triangle]
For all $X,Y$, \;$\aa_{X,\mathbf 1,Y}\circ(\id_X\otimes \lambda_Y)=\rho_X\otimes \id_Y.$
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The causal loop closes to the identity]\label{thm:loop}
For objects $W,X,Y,Z$ in a thin monoidal category, travelling from
$((W\otimes X)\otimes Y)\otimes Z$ to $W\otimes(X\otimes(Y\otimes Z))$ along the long side of
the pentagon and returning along the inverse of the short side yields the identity:
\[
\Big[(\aa_{W,X,Y}\otimes\id_Z)\circ\aa_{W,X\otimes Y,Z}\circ(\id_W\otimes\aa_{X,Y,Z})\Big]
\circ\Big[\aa_{W\otimes X,Y,Z}\circ\aa_{W,X,Y\otimes Z}\Big]^{-1}=\id.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both sides are endomorphisms of a common object; by thinness they are equal, and the identity
is such an endomorphism. Equivalently, apply Corollary~\ref{cor:pent} and cancel.
\end{proof}

\section{The parenthesization category}

Fix a set $\alpha$ of leaf labels.

\begin{definition}[Parenthesization trees]
The set $\PTree(\alpha)$ is generated inductively by an empty tree $\nil$, a leaf $\leaf(a)$ for
$a\in\alpha$, and a node $\node(s,t)$ for trees $s,t$, read as the bracketed product $(s\cdot
t)$. The flattening map $\flt\colon\PTree(\alpha)\to\mathrm{List}(\alpha)$ is
\[
\flt(\nil)=[\,],\quad \flt(\leaf(a))=[a],\quad \flt(\node(s,t))=\flt(s)\mathbin{+\!\!+}\flt(t).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The parenthesization category]
Objects are parenthesization trees; the hom-set $\PC(s,t)$ is the (subsingleton) set of proofs
of $\flt(s)=\flt(t)$. Composition is transitivity of equality and identities are reflexivity.
This is the parenthesization category $\PC(\alpha)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Thin groupoid]\label{thm:thin}
$\PC(\alpha)$ is thin, and every morphism is invertible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Hom-sets are proofs of a single equation, hence subsingletons. A proof $\flt(s)=\flt(t)$ has a
symmetric proof $\flt(t)=\flt(s)$; the composites are identities by thinness.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Tensor datum]
On $\PC(\alpha)$: tensor $s\otimes t:=\node(s,t)$; unit $\mathbf 1:=\nil$; whiskering by left or
right concatenation of the flattened data; associator $\aa_{a,b,c}$ from associativity of list
concatenation; unitors $\lambda_a,\rho_a$ from $[\,]\mathbin{+\!\!+}\ell=\ell$ and
$\ell\mathbin{+\!\!+}[\,]=\ell$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Monoidal category]
With this datum, $\PC(\alpha)$ is a monoidal category; the pentagon, triangle and all naturality
axioms hold.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\PC(\alpha)$ is thin (Theorem~\ref{thm:thin}), so Theorem~\ref{thm:free} supplies every axiom.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Associativity fails on the nose]\label{thm:ne}
For all trees $a,b,c$, the objects $(a\otimes b)\otimes c$ and $a\otimes(b\otimes c)$ are
distinct.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\node(\node(a,b),c)=\node(a,\node(b,c))$ then comparing first components gives
$\node(a,b)=a$, so a tree equals a proper subtree of itself; comparing sizes,
$\mathrm{size}(\node(a,b))=\mathrm{size}(a)+\mathrm{size}(b)+1>\mathrm{size}(a)$, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-strict]
$\PC(\alpha)$ is not strict: e.g. for $a=b=c=\nil$ the associator's source and target differ, so
it cannot be an identity.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the associator]
For all $a,b,c$, the associator $\aa_{a,b,c}$ is the unique isomorphism
$(a\otimes b)\otimes c\cong a\otimes(b\otimes c)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By thinness and Proposition~\ref{prop:isouniq} there is at most one such isomorphism, and
$\aa_{a,b,c}$ is one.
\end{proof}

\section{Strictification: collapsing the loops}

\begin{definition}[Normal form]
For a word $\ell$ define the right-nested tree $\ofList([\,])=\nil$,
$\ofList(a::\ell')=\node(\leaf(a),\ofList(\ell'))$. Then $\flt(\ofList(\ell))=\ell$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Normalization]\label{thm:norm}
Every tree $s$ is canonically (and, by thinness, uniquely) isomorphic to
$\ofList(\flt(s))$. All bracketings of a fixed word are uniquely isomorphic --- Mac Lane
coherence in concrete form.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\flt(\ofList(\flt(s)))=\flt(s)$ gives a morphism $s\to\ofList(\flt(s))$, an isomorphism
because $\PC(\alpha)$ is a groupoid; uniqueness is Proposition~\ref{prop:isouniq}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Isomorphism criterion]
Trees $s,t$ are isomorphic iff $\flt(s)=\flt(t)$. The isomorphism class remembers only the
word.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An isomorphism yields a morphism $s\to t$, i.e. a proof $\flt(s)=\flt(t)$; conversely such a
proof is an invertible morphism (Theorem~\ref{thm:thin}).
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Flattening functor]
Let $\Disc(\mathrm{List}(\alpha))$ be the discrete category on words. The flattening functor
$F\colon\PC(\alpha)\to\Disc(\mathrm{List}(\alpha))$ sends $s\mapsto\flt(s)$ and each morphism to
the corresponding identity-type morphism.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The loop is contracted]
$F(\aa_{a,b,c})$ is the identity on $\flt(a)\mathbin{+\!\!+}\flt(b)\mathbin{+\!\!+}\flt(c)$:
strictification unbends the associator loop.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Discrete hom-sets are subsingletons, so parallel morphisms coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strictification]
The flattening functor is an equivalence $\PC(\alpha)\simeq\Disc(\mathrm{List}(\alpha))$, with
inverse $G=\ofList$. The non-strict structure is, up to equivalence, the strict discrete
category of words.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$F\circ G$ is the identity on objects since $\flt(\ofList(\ell))=\ell$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:norm}, $s\cong\ofList(\flt(s))=(G\circ F)(s)$ naturally in $s$, so
$\id\cong G\circ F$; naturality and the triangle identities are automatic by thinness of the
source and discreteness of the target, giving an adjoint equivalence.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The lesson is that \emph{rigidity guarantees coherence}: when re-association transformations are
unique, as in any thin category, the pentagon, triangle and naturality laws hold for free and
the non-strict structure is equivalent to a strict one. The parenthesization category makes both
halves concrete: associativity fails as an equation of objects, yet its repair is unique and
coherence is free.

Natural extensions include: upgrading the equivalence to a \emph{monoidal} equivalence with
concatenation as tensor on words (full strictification for this family); a detailed treatment of
unit coherence via the triangle; a bicategorical delooping producing an explicit one-object
bicategory with non-associative horizontal composition; and the study of non-thin obstructions
where the pentagon genuinely fails, quantifying how thinness removes the obstruction.

\section{Conclusion}

From nothing but parenthesizations we obtained a monoidal category in which associativity fails
on the nose but is repaired by a canonical, unique associator, with all coherence automatic ---
and which collapses, up to equivalence, to the strict discrete category of words. It is a
complete miniature of the theory: failure, canonical repair, free coherence, strictification.

\end{document}

